(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the principle and necessity of sectional views in engineering drawings, detailing how section planes eliminate visual confusion caused by overlapping hidden lines.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Principle and Need for Sectional Views:) Tj
T*
(   - In standard orthographic projection, internal features \(such as stepped bores, counterbores,) Tj
T*
(   keyways, oil channels, cavities, and ribs\) are represented by Dashed Thin Lines \(hidden lines,) Tj
T*
(   Type E/F\).) Tj
T*
(   - In complex components \(such as engine cylinder blocks, pump casings, gearbox housings, and) Tj
T*
(   multi-port valves\), internal geometries overlap extensively in external views.) Tj
T*
(   - The resulting proliferation of crossing and overlapping dashed lines creates severe visual) Tj
T*
(   confusion, obscures critical wall thicknesses, and makes accurate dimensioning nearly) Tj
T*
(   impossible.) Tj
T*
(   - Sectional views resolve this problem by imagining an imaginary cutting plane that slices) Tj
T*
(   through the object, mentally discarding the material between the observer and the plane to) Tj
T*
(   reveal the internal construction as crisp VISIBLE OUTLINES \(Type A, continuous thick lines\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mechanics of Mental Sectioning:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: An imaginary cutting plane \(section plane\) is passed through the chosen feature axis.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: The portion of the component situated between the observer and the cutting plane is) Tj
T*
(   mentally removed.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: The remaining portion is projected orthographically onto the parallel projection) Tj
T*
(   plane.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: The surfaces where the imaginary cutting plane physically cut through solid material) Tj
T*
(   are shaded with standardized Section Lining \(Hatching\).) Tj
T*
(   - Crucial Note: Mental sectioning is purely an illustrative convention; the physical component) Tj
T*
(   is NOT destroyed or modified in reality.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the representation and conventions of Cutting Plane lines according to BIS SP 46:2003 \(ISO 128\), explaining line thickness, dash-dot patterns, viewing direction arrows, and reference lettering.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Cutting Plane Line Specification \(Type H Line\):) Tj
T*
(   - Pattern: Chain thin line \(long dash-dot pattern: long dash 10-12 mm, gap 1 mm, short dash/dot) Tj
T*
(   1.5 mm, gap 1 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   - Thickness Variations:) Tj
T*
(   * The interior body of the cutting plane line is drawn as a CONTINUOUS THIN line \(~0.25 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * The TERMINATING ENDS and any POINTS OF DIRECTION CHANGE \(bends\) are drawn as THICK lines) Tj
T*
(   \(~0.50 mm, Grade H/HB\) of length 5 mm to 10 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Significance: The thick ends immediately catch the eye of the machinist, while the thin) Tj
T*
(   interior body prevents visual interference with underlying part outlines.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Viewing Direction Arrows:) Tj
T*
(   - Terminating at each thick end is a large, closed and filled arrowhead.) Tj
T*
(   - Orientation: The arrowheads are oriented STRICTLY PERPENDICULAR to the cutting plane line,) Tj
T*
(   pointing directly in the line of sight of the observer.) Tj
T*
(   - Placement: Positioned 2 mm to 3 mm away from the thick end, with the point of the arrow) Tj
T*
(   touching the line or placed directly on the end.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Reference Lettering and Title Designation:) Tj
T*
(   - Identification Letters: Large uppercase gothic letters \(height 5 mm to 7 mm, e.g. 'A', 'B',) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
