(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the rules and conventions for Section Lining \(Hatching\) according to BIS SP 46, detailing line types, angles of inclination, spacing, adjacent components, and thin section representations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Hatching Line Standards \(Type B Line\):) Tj
T*
(   - Line Type: Continuous thin lines \(~0.25 mm, Grade 2H/3H\).) Tj
T*
(   - Standard Angle: Drawn at an inclination of 45 degrees relative to the principal centerline or) Tj
T*
(   base outline of the component.) Tj
T*
(   - Spacing: Uniformly spaced across the entire cut area. Pitch ranges from 1.5 mm to 3 mm) Tj
T*
(   depending on the overall size of the surface \(never drawn too close to prevent blurring, nor) Tj
T*
(   too wide to maintain a distinct texture\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Special Angle Rules:) Tj
T*
(   - If the main outlines of the component or principal centerlines are themselves inclined at 45) Tj
T*
(   degrees to the horizontal, hatching at 45? would run parallel to the edges, creating visual) Tj
T*
(   ambiguity.) Tj
T*
(   - In such cases, hatching lines MUST be turned to an angle of 30 degrees or 60 degrees relative) Tj
T*
(   to the baseline!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Hatching in Assembly Drawings \(Adjacent Parts\):) Tj
T*
(   - To distinguish mating components in mechanical assemblies \(e.g., shaft inside bushing inside) Tj
T*
(   housing\):) Tj
T*
(   * Component 1: Hatched at 45 degrees sloping upwards to the right.) Tj
T*
(   * Component 2: Hatched at 135 degrees \(45 degrees sloping downwards to the right\).) Tj
T*
(   * Component 3: Hatched at 45? or 30? with a distinctly DIFFERENT line spacing \(finer or coarser) Tj
T*
(   pitch\), or lines staggered with an offset.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Representation of Thin Sections \(< 2 mm\):) Tj
T*
(   - For structural sheet metal, gaskets, shims, and seals where line hatching is physically) Tj
T*
(   impractical:) Tj
T*
(   * The cross-section is filled COMPLETELY SOLID BLACK.) Tj
T*
(   * Where adjacent thin components meet, a distinct white gap of 1 mm to 1.5 mm is left between) Tj
T*
(   them to show the joint interface.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare and contrast the six primary types of sectional views: Full Section, Half Section, Offset Section, Revolved Section, Removed Section, and Broken-Out Section.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Full Sectional View:) Tj
T*
(   - The cutting plane extends completely across the component along a continuous straight path.) Tj
T*
(   - Used for general non-symmetrical hollow objects, valves, and housings to expose the complete) Tj
T*
(   internal profile from end to end.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Half Sectional View:) Tj
T*
(   - The cutting plane cuts through only ONE-QUARTER of a symmetrical object, stopping at the) Tj
T*
(   central axis.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
