(   - One half of the view displays internal features in section; the other half displays external) Tj
T*
(   visible contours.) Tj
T*
(   - The two halves are separated by a central CHAIN THIN CENTERLINE \(never a solid line\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Offset Sectional View:) Tj
T*
(   - The cutting plane is stepped or bent at 90-degree right angles to pass through non-collinear) Tj
T*
(   holes, slots, or cavities.) Tj
T*
(   - The 90? steps are NOT shown in the sectional view; the cut appears as a continuous flat) Tj
T*
(   plane.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Revolved \(Rotated\) Section:) Tj
T*
(   - A cross-section of a structural feature \(rib, spoke, arm, bar\) is cut by a plane) Tj
T*
(   perpendicular to its axis, rotated 90 degrees in place, and drawn directly on the existing) Tj
T*
(   view.) Tj
T*
(   - Drawn with continuous thin lines \(Type B\) without breaking the view.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   5. Removed Section:) Tj
T*
(   - A cross-section cut, rotated 90 degrees, and placed OUTSIDE the existing view \(often adjacent) Tj
T*
(   or on an extension of the cutting plane line\) and labeled 'SECTION A-A'.) Tj
T*
(   - Drawn with standard continuous thick outlines \(Type A\). Can be drawn at an enlarged scale.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   6. Broken-Out \(Partial / Local\) Section:) Tj
T*
(   - Used when only a small localized interior detail needs inspection \(e.g. a keyway, set-screw) Tj
T*
(   hole\).) Tj
T*
(   - A small section is revealed by an imaginary break line drawn as a continuous thin freehand) Tj
T*
(   wavy line \(Type C\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the rules governing components and features that are EXEMPT from longitudinal sectioning \(non-sectioned parts\) according to BIS SP 46 and ISO 128, explaining the engineering rationale.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Non-Sectioning Rule \(Longitudinal Cut\):) Tj
T*
(   When a cutting plane passes longitudinally along the axis or centerline of certain standard) Tj
T*
(   machine elements, they are shown in EXTERNAL ELEVATION \(unhatched and unsectioned\), even though) Tj
T*
(   the cutting plane physically slices through them!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Catalog of Non-Sectioned Components:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Solid Shafts, Spindles, and Axles.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Standard Fasteners: Bolts, Studs, Screws, Nuts, and Washers.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Locking & Alignment Elements: Keys, Cotters, Taper Pins, Split Pins, Dowels.) Tj
T*
(   - 4. Structural Connectors: Solid Rivets.) Tj
T*
(   - 5. Transmission Elements: Gear Teeth, Roller Chains, Ball Bearings, Roller Bearings.) Tj
T*
(   - 6. Structural Features: Thin Webs, Ribs, Gussets, and Spokes of wheels or pulleys.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Rationale:) Tj
T*
(   - Lack of Internal Complexity: These components are solid entities. Sectioning a solid shaft or) Tj
T*
(   bolt reveals nothing inside except solid metal, while creating excessive visual clutter and) Tj
T*
(   drafting labor.) Tj
T*
(   - Prevention of False Impressions:) Tj
T*
(   * If a thin rib or web is hatched longitudinally, it creates the false optical impression that) Tj
T*
(   the entire casting is a massive solid block of metal, obscuring its lightweight ribbed) Tj
T*
(   construction.) Tj
T*
(   * If spokes of a pulley are hatched, the view falsely resembles a solid webbed flywheel disk!) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
