(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A cast iron flanged pipe joint assembly connects two pipes of 80 mm bore, 100 mm outer diameter, and 180 mm flange diameter \(flange thickness 25 mm\). The flanges are connected by four 16 mm bolts on a PCD of 140 mm with a 3 mm gasket between flanges. Explain how this assembly is represented in a Full Sectional Elevation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Assembly Component Configuration:) Tj
T*
(   - Pipe Bore: Inside diameter = 80 mm \(Radius = 40 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   - Pipe Body Wall: Outer diameter = 100 mm \(Wall thickness = 10 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   - Flange Bases: Outer diameter = 180 mm, Thickness = 25 mm each.) Tj
T*
(   - Gasket: Outer diameter = 180 mm, Bore = 80 mm, Thickness = 3 mm \(positioned between flange) Tj
T*
(   faces\).) Tj
T*
(   - Bolts: Four M16 hexagonal bolts on PCD = 140 mm \(Radius = 70 mm from center\).) Tj
T*
(   - Longitudinal Cutting Plane passes along the pipe axis and through two diametrically opposite) Tj
T*
(   bolt holes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Sectional Elevation Drafting Breakdown:) Tj
T*
(   - Central Axis: Chain thin centerline along the pipe bore axis \(Z = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Pipe 1 \(Left Flange & Pipe Body\):) Tj
T*
(   * Cut surfaces include the upper pipe wall \(from R = 40 to 50 mm, and flange to R = 90 mm\) and) Tj
T*
(   lower pipe wall \(from R = -40 to -50 mm, and flange to R = -90 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Hatching: Hatched on layer 'HATCH' using ANSI31 inclined at 45 degrees sloping upwards to the) Tj
T*
(   right.) Tj
T*
(   - Pipe 2 \(Right Flange & Pipe Body\):) Tj
T*
(   * Cut surfaces are identical in geometry on the right side of the gasket.) Tj
T*
(   * Hatching: Hatched using ANSI31 inclined at 135 degrees \(sloping downwards to the right\) to) Tj
T*
(   clearly demarcate the component boundary from Pipe 1!) Tj
T*
(   - Gasket \(3 mm thick\):) Tj
T*
(   * Because thickness is only 3 mm, it is drawn filled SOLID BLACK \(or densely hatched with) Tj
T*
(   cross-mesh\) with a clear outline.) Tj
T*
(   - M16 Bolts & Nuts \(at R = +70 mm and R = -70 mm\):) Tj
T*
(   * The bolts pass through 18 mm clearance holes in the flanges.) Tj
T*
(   * NON-SECTIONING RULE APPLIES: The bolt shanks, hexagonal heads, and nuts are drawn as VISIBLE) Tj
T*
(   OUTLINES \(UNHATCHED\) in external elevation!) Tj
T*
(   * The 18 mm clearance space between bolt shank and hole wall is visible on either side of the) Tj
T*
(   bolt.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Half Sectional View in exhaustive detail, including its construction methodology for symmetrical mechanical bodies, rules for dividing lines, and dimensioning protocols.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept and Applicability:) Tj
T*
(   - A Half Sectional View is utilized exclusively for SYMMETRICAL components \(such as pulleys,) Tj
T*
(   gear blanks, flanges, bushings, pistons, and wheels\).) Tj
T*
(   - The cutting plane comprises two perpendicular planes meeting at the central axis of symmetry) Tj
T*
(   \(cutting away one quadrant = 90? wedge\).) Tj
T*
(   - One half of the resulting view shows the exterior appearance of the part, while the other) Tj
T*
(   half reveals internal cavities and wall thicknesses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

