(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Offset Sectional View, detailing how the cutting plane path is defined in parent views, how 90-degree transitions are drafted, and how to represent non-collinear features.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Engineering Need for Offset Sectioning:) Tj
T*
(   - In complex components \(such as engine cylinder heads, pump bodies, machine brackets\),) Tj
T*
(   internal holes, slots, bosses, and counterbores rarely lie along a single straight line.) Tj
T*
(   - A single continuous straight cutting plane would miss most of these critical features.) Tj
T*
(   - An Offset Section solves this by stepping or jogging the cutting plane path at 90-degree) Tj
T*
(   right angles to pass through all desired features sequentially.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Representation on the Parent View \(Plan or Elevation\):) Tj
T*
(   - The stepped cutting plane path is drawn as a Type H line:) Tj
T*
(   * Continuous thin chain line along the path.) Tj
T*
(   * THICK LINE SEGMENTS \(~0.50 mm\) at the terminating ends AND AT EVERY 90-DEGREE BEND \(step\) in) Tj
T*
(   the path.) Tj
T*
(   * Viewing direction arrows and identification letters \(e.g., A-A\) at the ends.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Representation in the Sectional View:) Tj
T*
(   - CRUCIAL ISO/BIS RULE: The 90-degree stepped bends of the cutting plane are COMPLETELY) Tj
T*
(   INVISIBLE in the sectional view!) Tj
T*
(   - No lines \(solid, dashed, or chain\) are drawn at the locations where the cutting plane stepped) Tj
T*
(   from one offset to another.) Tj
T*
(   - The sectional view is drafted as if all cut features existed in a SINGLE CONTINUOUS FLAT) Tj
T*
(   PLANE.) Tj
T*
(   - The section lining \(hatching\) maintains the exact same angle and spacing across the entire) Tj
T*
(   cut face, flowing seamlessly across the steps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
