(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Revolved \(Rotated\) Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - Concept: A cutting plane is passed perpendicular to the longitudinal axis of an elongated) Tj
T*
(   feature \(such as a crane hook, spoke of a wheel, structural rib, or hand tool shank\).) Tj
T*
(   - The resulting cross-section is rotated 90 degrees IN PLACE and drawn directly superimposed on) Tj
T*
(   the existing view.) Tj
T*
(   - Drafting Conventions:) Tj
T*
(   * If the view of the object is continuous \(not broken\), the outline of the revolved section is) Tj
T*
(   drawn with CONTINUOUS THIN LINES \(Type B\) and hatched. The original object outlines continue) Tj
T*
(   uninterrupted underneath.) Tj
T*
(   * If the view of the object is broken open to receive the section, the cross-section is drawn) Tj
T*
(   between the break lines using CONTINUOUS THICK LINES \(Type A\).) Tj
T*
(   - Application: Immediate inspection of cross-sectional profiles of wrenches, connecting rod) Tj
T*
(   shanks, and machine tool handles.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Removed Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - Concept: The cross-section is cut by a perpendicular plane, rotated 90 degrees, and REMOVED) Tj
T*
(   from the view to be drawn elsewhere on the sheet.) Tj
T*
(   - Placement Options:) Tj
T*
(   * Placed adjacent to the cutting plane, connected to it by a chain thin centerline.) Tj
T*
(   * Placed in a convenient empty space on the sheet, explicitly labeled with a title: 'SECTION) Tj
T*
(   A-A' or 'SECTION B-B'.) Tj
T*
(   * Drawn at an ENLARGED SCALE \(e.g., Scale 5:1\) to show minute groove radii or thread details) Tj
T*
(   clearly \(Detail Section\).) Tj
T*
(   - Drafting Conventions:) Tj
T*
(   * Drawn with standard CONTINUOUS THICK OUTLINES \(Type A\) and fully hatched.) Tj
T*
(   - Application: Complex turbine blades, structural steel I-beams, ship hull frames, and) Tj
T*
(   automotive chassis rails.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
