(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the BREAK and JOIN commands in AutoCAD, explaining their operational syntax and specific applications in generating partial and broken-out sectional views.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. BREAK Command \(BR\):) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Cuts and removes a segment of an entity between two specified points, or splits an) Tj
T*
(   entity into two separate objects at a single point.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard Two-Point Break:) Tj
T*
(   * Command: BREAK [Enter] -> Select object \(the pick point acts as First Break Point\).) Tj
T*
(   * Prompt: 'Specify second break point or [First point]:' -> Pick second point.) Tj
T*
(   * The segment between the two points is erased, creating a gap.) Tj
T*
(   - Single-Point Split \(@ Syntax\):) Tj
T*
(   * To split a line at a specific intersection without creating an empty gap:) Tj
T*
(   * Command: BREAK -> Select line -> Type 'F' \(First point\) [Enter] -> Snap to intersection ->) Tj
T*
(   Type '@' [Enter].) Tj
T*
(   * The line is cleanly divided into two separate entities touching at that exact coordinate!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. JOIN Command \(J\):) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Merges two or more collinear lines, coplanar arcs, elliptical arcs, splines, or) Tj
T*
(   polylines into a single continuous entity.) Tj
T*
(   - Syntax: Command: JOIN [Enter] -> Select source object -> Select objects to join -> [Enter].) Tj
T*
(   - If two lines are collinear but separated by a gap \(e.g. from an accidental break\), JOIN) Tj
T*
(   bridges the gap with a continuous straight line segment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Application in Broken-Out Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - In broken-out sections, the outer visible outline of a casting must be interrupted to insert) Tj
T*
(   the freehand break curve \(Type C\).) Tj
T*
(   - BREAK is used to split the outer visible line at the break curve endpoints, and the isolated) Tj
T*
(   interior segment is converted to the 'HIDDEN' layer or deleted.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 15:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Aligned Sectional View, detailing its construction for circular flanges with an odd number of holes or radial arms and contrasting it with standard offset sections.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem of Asymmetrical Radial Features:) Tj
T*
(   - In circular components such as pump impellers, flange couplings, handwheels, and brake drums,) Tj
T*
(   features \(such as bolt holes, ribs, or spokes\) are frequently arranged symmetrically at odd) Tj
T*
(   angular intervals \(e.g., 3 holes spaced at 120?, or 5 spokes spaced at 72?\).) Tj
T*
(   - A single flat cutting plane passed along the primary centerline would cut through one hole on) Tj
T*
(   one side, but would miss the holes on the opposite side completely, passing through solid metal) Tj
T*
(   instead.) Tj
T*
(   - Projecting this directly would create an asymmetrical, misleading view suggesting the part is) Tj
T*
(   out of balance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Principle of Aligned Sectioning:) Tj
T*
(   - In an Aligned Section, the cutting plane is bent to pass through one of the angled features) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., bent at 60? to pass through an offset hole\).) Tj
T*
(   - ROTATION RULE: Before projecting into the sectional view, the inclined portion of the cutting) Tj
T*
(   plane \(and the feature it cuts\) is IMAGINED TO BE ROTATED about the central axis of symmetry) Tj
T*
(   until it becomes COLLINEAR with the primary cutting plane!) Tj
T*
(   - The view is then projected as a straight, symmetrical section.) Tj
T*
ET

