(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the representation of fasteners \(bolts, studs, screws, pins, keys\) in sectional assembly drawings, explaining when they are shown in full elevation and when they are hatched.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Rules for Fasteners in Longitudinal Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - When an assembly cutting plane passes along the longitudinal axis of standard fasteners:) Tj
T*
(   * Hexagonal Bolts, Studs, Machine Screws, Cap Screws.) Tj
T*
(   * Nuts and Washers.) Tj
T*
(   * Knuckle Pins, Cotters, Taper Pins, Dowel Pins.) Tj
T*
(   * Parallel Keys, Woodruff Keys.) Tj
T*
(   - MANDATORY CONVENTION: All such fasteners are shown in FULL EXTERNAL ELEVATION \(UNSECTIONED /) Tj
T*
(   UNHATCHED\)!) Tj
T*
(   - Their outer visible outlines, thread runouts, and chamfer bevels are drawn with continuous) Tj
T*
(   thick lines \(Type A\).) Tj
T*
(   - The thread root is drawn as a continuous thin line \(Type B\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Clearance Representation:) Tj
T*
(   - Standard bolt holes are drilled slightly larger than the nominal bolt diameter \(clearance) Tj
T*
(   hole, e.g. 18 mm hole for M16 bolt\).) Tj
T*
(   - In the sectional view, this clearance gap is shown as an unhatched white space between the) Tj
T*
(   outer solid contour of the bolt shank and the hatched inner wall of the flange hole.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. When Fasteners ARE Hatched:) Tj
T*
(   - Transverse Cut: If the cutting plane cuts across the fastener PERPENDICULAR to its axis, the) Tj
T*
(   resulting circular or rectangular cross-section is hatched normally.) Tj
T*
(   - Hollow Fasteners: If a bolt or pin is hollow \(e.g., hollow banjo bolt for hydraulic fluid\),) Tj
T*
(   the hollow bore is shown in section, and the annular metal wall IS HATCHED!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
