12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the methods of sectioning thin-walled structures \(ribs, webs, gussets\) cut transversely versus longitudinally, illustrating with a cast iron T-bracket.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Rib Sectioning Paradox:) Tj
T*
(   - A structural rib \(or web\) is a thin, plate-like reinforcing member designed to stiffen a) Tj
T*
(   casting against bending moments without adding excessive weight.) Tj
T*
(   - The representation of a rib depends entirely on whether the cutting plane cuts it) Tj
T*
(   LONGITUDINALLY or TRANSVERSELY.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Longitudinal Sectioning of a Rib \(Cutting Plane Parallel to Flat Face\):) Tj
T*
(   - When the cutting plane passes along the longitudinal center plane of the rib:) Tj
T*
(   * THE RIB IS LEFT UNSECTIONED \(UNHATCHED\)!) Tj
T*
(   * The boundary of the rib is drawn as a continuous thick visible outline.) Tj
T*
(   * Rationale: If the rib were hatched, the sectional view would show a massive solid triangular) Tj
T*
(   block of metal, completely disguising the fact that the rib is merely a thin 10 mm stiffener!) Tj
T*
(   - Alternative ISO Convention \(Alternate Spacing\): Some standards permit hatching the) Tj
T*
(   longitudinal rib with DOUBLE the line spacing \(e.g. 6 mm pitch instead of 3 mm\) to indicate its) Tj
T*
(   thin nature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Transverse Sectioning of a Rib \(Cutting Plane Perpendicular to Flat Face\):) Tj
T*
(   - When the cutting plane cuts across the rib perpendicular to its length:) Tj
T*
(   * THE RIB IS FULLY HATCHED!) Tj
T*
(   * The exposed rectangular or trapezoidal cross-section is hatched with standard 45-degree) Tj
T*
(   section lining like any other solid cut feature.) Tj
T*
(   * In this orientation, hatching accurately displays the true thickness and height of the) Tj
T*
(   stiffener without visual ambiguity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 22:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the representation of gears, bearings, and springs in sectional drawings, detailing conventional simplifications adopted in engineering practice.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Sectional Representation of Spur Gears \(ISO 2203\):) Tj
T*
(   - Gear teeth are NEVER sectioned longitudinally!) Tj
T*
(   - When a cutting plane passes through the axis of a gear:) Tj
T*
(   * The root cylinder \(bottom of teeth\) is drawn as a CONTINUOUS THICK LINE \(Type A\).) Tj
T*
(   * The tip cylinder \(outside diameter\) is drawn as a CONTINUOUS THICK LINE.) Tj
T*
(   * The pitch cylinder diameter \(PCD\) is drawn as a CHAIN THIN LINE \(Type G\).) Tj
T*
(   * The gear hub and web are hatched, but the gear tooth in the section plane is shown in FULL) Tj
T*
(   ELEVATION \(UNHATCHED\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Sectional Representation of Rolling Contact Bearings:) Tj
T*
(   - In assembly section drawings, drawing every ball, roller, cage, and raceway fillet is) Tj
T*
(   excessively tedious.) Tj
T*
ET

