(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Hatch Boundary Leak detection tools in AutoCAD, discussing Gap Tolerance \(HPGAPTOL\), red circle glyphs, and methods to resolve non-closed loops.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of Boundary Leaks:) Tj
T*
(   - When using 'Pick internal point' in the HATCH command, AutoCAD requires a mathematically) Tj
T*
(   closed perimeter enclosing the point.) Tj
T*
(   - If two lines fail to meet by even 0.001 mm \(an invisible micro-gap caused by manual drafting) Tj
T*
(   without OSNAP\), the ray-casting algorithm 'leaks' out into the surrounding canvas.) Tj
T*
(   - AutoCAD aborts the operation and displays the error message: 'A closed boundary cannot be) Tj
T*
(   determined.') Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Visual Leak Diagnostics \(Red Circles\):) Tj
T*
(   - In modern AutoCAD, when a leak occurs, the software automatically places two bright RED) Tj
T*
(   CIRCULAR GLYPHS on the screen directly circling the open gap vertices!) Tj
T*
(   - This pinpoints the exact physical location of the boundary defect instantly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Resolution Methods:) Tj
T*
(   - Method 1 \(Geometric Fillet Repair\):) Tj
T*
(   * Zoom into the red glyph. Command: FILLET -> Radius = 0 -> Select the two open lines. AutoCAD) Tj
T*
(   immediately extends or trims them to a perfect closed vertex.) Tj
T*
(   - Method 2 \(Gap Tolerance - HPGAPTOL\):) Tj
T*
(   * If a drawing contains dozens of tiny drafting gaps:) Tj
T*
(   * Command: HPGAPTOL [Enter] -> Set value \(e.g. '1.0' mm\).) Tj
T*
(   * AutoCAD will treat any gap equal to or smaller than 1.0 mm as closed, applying the hatch) Tj
T*
(   successfully while warning the user.) Tj
T*
(   - Method 3 \(Boundary Creation - BO\):) Tj
T*
(   * Use the BOUNDARY command to generate a closed polyline boundary before hatching.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the methods of creating custom hatch patterns in AutoCAD, detailing the structure of the .pat file, delta-x, delta-y, and line dash-gap specifications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Anatomy of the AutoCAD Hatch Pattern File \(.pat\):) Tj
T*
(   - Custom hatch patterns are saved as plain text files with a '.pat' extension \(e.g.) Tj
T*
(   'custom.pat'\) or appended to 'acad.pat'.) Tj
T*
(   - Each pattern definition consists of a header line followed by one or more pattern line) Tj
T*
(   descriptors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Syntax Structure:) Tj
T*
(   - Header Line:) Tj
T*
(   *pattern-name, description) Tj
T*
(   Example: *BRICK_CUSTOM, Standard running brick pattern) Tj
T*
(   - Pattern Line Descriptor Syntax:) Tj
T*
(   angle, x-origin, y-origin, delta-x, delta-y, dash-1, dash-2, ...) Tj
T*
(   * angle: Inclination of lines in degrees \(0 to 360\).) Tj
T*
(   * x-origin, y-origin: Coordinates of base anchor point for the pattern family.) Tj
T*
(   * delta-x: Offset of successive line segments along the line direction.) Tj
T*
(   * delta-y: Perpendicular spacing between parallel lines in the family.) Tj
T*
(   * dash-1, dash-2: Sequence of lengths \(positive = pen down / dash; negative = pen up / space; 0) Tj
T*
(   = dot\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Practical Example \(Simple 45? Steel Hatch\):) Tj
T*
(   *STEEL_45, Pairs of 45 degree lines) Tj
T*
(   45, 0, 0, 0, 6, 10, 0) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
